&lt;p>&lt;strong>Part 24 of the &lt;em>Learning Quantum Physics&lt;/em> series.&lt;/strong>&lt;/p>
&lt;p>X, Y, and Z are easy to draw. They are harder to hold in mind together when a plane, a turn, and a qubit all refer to them.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>This article gives each axis a color and passes three colored planes through a sphere. The colors keep the axes distinct. The sphere shows what a turn does. The same six directions then name the states of one qubit.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>